  cta={{ href: '/klondike/', label: 'Play Klondike Solitaire' }}
>
  <p>
    Klondike is the game most people simply call "Solitaire." It is a one-player
    <strong>patience</strong> game played with a single deck, and the version that shipped with
    early home computers made it the most-played card game in the world. This page is a complete
    reference to the rules: how the cards are dealt, what you are trying to do, exactly which moves
    are legal, how the stock and scoring work, and how a game ends. If you would rather learn by
    doing, you can <a href="/klondike/">play a hand</a> right away.
  </p>

  <h2>The setup</h2>
  <p>
    Klondike uses one <strong>standard 52-card deck</strong> (no Jokers). After shuffling, the
    cards are dealt into four areas of the table:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The tableau</strong> — seven columns dealt left to right. The first column gets one
      card, the second two, the third three, and so on up to seven in the last column — 28 cards
      in all. In every column, only the <strong>top (last-dealt) card is face-up</strong>; all the
      cards beneath it are face-down.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The foundations</strong> — four empty spaces, one for each suit. They start the game
      with no cards and are built up over the course of play.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The stock</strong> — the 24 cards left over after the deal, placed face-down as a
      draw pile.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The waste</strong> — an empty pile next to the stock that receives the cards you turn
      over from it.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The objective</h2>
  <p>
    You win by building all four <strong>foundations</strong>. Each foundation holds one suit and
    is built <strong>up in sequence, starting with the Ace</strong>: Ace, then Two, Three, and so
    on through Jack, Queen, and finally King. When all four foundations are complete — 13 cards
    each, 52 in total — the game is won.
  </p>

  <h2>Legal moves</h2>
  <p>
    On your turn you may make any move that the rules allow, in any order, for as long as legal
    moves exist. There are five kinds:
  </p>
  <h3>Building on the tableau</h3>
  <p>
    You build the tableau columns <strong>downward in rank and in alternating colours</strong>. A
    card may be placed on a tableau card that is one rank higher and the opposite colour — for
    example, a red Nine goes on a black Ten, and a black Queen goes on a red King. (Red suits are
    Hearts and Diamonds; black suits are Clubs and Spades.)
  </p>
  <h3>Moving runs as a unit</h3>
  <p>
    A sequence of face-up cards that is already in correct order (descending, alternating colour)
    is called a <strong>run</strong>, and you may move the whole run at once. The card at the
    bottom of the run still has to follow the alternating-colour rule for wherever it lands.
    Face-down cards can never be moved.
  </p>
  <h3>Filling an empty column</h3>
  <p>
    When a tableau column is emptied, only a <strong>King</strong> — alone, or as the top of a run
    led by a King — may be moved into the empty space. No other card can start a new column.
  </p>
  <h3>Playing to the foundations</h3>
  <p>
    The top card of any tableau column, or the top card of the waste, can be moved to a foundation
    whenever it is the next card that foundation needs (the matching suit and the next rank up).
    Only <strong>one card at a time</strong> may go to a foundation. You may also move a card back
    <em>off</em> a foundation onto the tableau if it helps, though this is rarely useful and costs
    points (see scoring).
  </p>
  <h3>Flipping an exposed card</h3>
  <p>
    Whenever a move uncovers a face-down card at the bottom of a tableau column, that card is
    <strong>turned face-up</strong> and becomes available to play. On CardHearth this happens
    automatically.
  </p>

  <h2>Drawing and recycling the stock</h2>
  <p>
    When you run out of useful tableau moves, you turn cards from the <strong>stock</strong> into
    the <strong>waste</strong>. In <strong>Draw 1</strong> you turn one card at a time; in
    <a href="/klondike-draw-3/"><strong>Draw 3</strong></a> you turn three at a time, and only the
    top card of those three is immediately playable. The top of the waste is always available to
    play onto the tableau or a foundation.
  </p>
  <p>
    When the stock is empty, you can <strong>recycle</strong>: the waste is turned face-down and
    becomes the stock again, so you can pass through the cards once more. Draw 1 allows unlimited
    recycles, and so does Draw 3 — though, as the scoring below shows, recycling is not free.
  </p>

  <h2>Scoring</h2>
  <p>
    CardHearth uses the standard Klondike scoring system. Points are awarded and deducted as
    follows:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>+10</strong> for every card moved to a foundation.</li>
    <li><strong>+5</strong> for moving a card from the waste to a tableau column.</li>
    <li><strong>+5</strong> for flipping a face-down tableau card face-up.</li>
    <li><strong>−15</strong> for moving a card back from a foundation to the tableau.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Recycling the waste:</strong> <strong>−100</strong> points each time in Draw 1, and
      <strong>−20</strong> points per pass in Draw 3 — but only starting from the
      <strong>third</strong> recycle onward (the first two Draw 3 passes are free). Your score
      never drops below zero from a recycle.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Moving a card from one tableau column to another scores nothing in itself — but if it uncovers
    a face-down card, you collect the +5 for the flip.
  </p>

  <h2>How you win — and when a game ends</h2>
  <p>
    You <strong>win</strong> the moment all 52 cards sit on the four foundations, each suit
    complete from Ace to King. Often the game reaches a point where every card is face-up and the
    stock and waste are empty; from there the outcome is decided, and CardHearth can
    <strong>auto-finish</strong> the deal for you.
  </p>
  <p>
    A game <strong>ends without a win</strong> when no legal move remains — no card can go to a
    foundation, no tableau move helps, and cycling the stock turns up nothing playable. Not every
    Klondike deal is solvable, so a dead end is part of the game. On CardHearth you can use
    unlimited <em>Undo</em> to back out of a wrong turn, or start a fresh deal.
  </p>

  <h2>Common variations</h2>
  <p>
    The two most common variations both appear on CardHearth. <strong>Draw 1 vs Draw 3</strong>
    changes how many stock cards you turn at once: Draw 1 is the gentler, higher-winning game,
    while Draw 3 demands you plan your stock passes. <strong>Vegas scoring</strong> is a different
    money-style system in which you "buy" the deck for 52 points and earn 5 points per card sent
    to a foundation, aiming to finish ahead — a contrast to the standard cumulative scoring
    described above. The rules of play are otherwise identical.
  </p>

  <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
  {
    faq.map((f) => (
      <details class="faq">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold">{f.q}</summary>
        <p class="mt-2 opacity-90">{f.a}</p>
      </details>
    ))
  }

  <h2>Keep going</h2>
  <p>
    Now that you know the rules, the next step is playing better. Read
    <a href="/guides/how-to-win-klondike-solitaire/">how to win Klondike Solitaire</a> for the
    habits that lift your win rate, brush up on terms in the
    <a href="/guides/solitaire-glossary/">Solitaire glossary</a>, or just
    <a href="/klondike/">play Klondike Solitaire</a> now. Our
    <a href="/guides/">guides hub</a> covers the rest of the CardHearth library.
  </p>
</Article>
